      "Saturday is for diagnosis in motion. You find out what is actually broken. Sit a short, timed set: five questions, one extract, one calculation sheet. Mark it the same morning. Circle the error type: misread command word, missing unit, no quotation, arithmetic, blank. The circle is the Sunday plan. If you skip marking, Saturday was a performance of studying and Sunday will have nothing to aim at.",
      "Protect Saturday from a heroic start time you will not keep. If you never begin at 6 a.m., do not print 6 a.m. to impress yourself. Choose the first hour that already exists in the house. A late honest start beats an early fictional one that sours the weekend by 9 a.m.",
      "Protect Saturday from a heroic start time you will not keep. If you never begin at 6 a.m., do not print 6 a.m. on a timetable to impress yourself. Choose the first hour that already exists in your house: after chores, after the younger sibling is settled, after the power usually comes. A late honest start beats an early fictional one. Fictional starts sour the whole weekend by 9 a.m.",
      "Build chores and worship and matches into the plan so they are not interruptions. Interruptions are what we call events we refused to schedule. If Saturday market is required, the study block is before or after, named on paper. If a football match is non-negotiable, it is a block with a start and an end, not a fog that eats the afternoon. Fog is the collapse mechanism.",
      "Sunday is practice of the circled errors, not a new syllabus. Redo the failed questions with the notes open once, then closed. Write one similar question yourself by changing a number or a name. If the weakness was an essay opening, write three openings and stop. Sunday that tries to finish the term will finish nothing. Finishing nothing is how Sunday night becomes a bargain with sleep. Set a finish line you can keep: 4 p.m. Sunday, books closed, bag packed for Monday, one page of what is still unfinished. The unfinished page is not a confession of failure. It is Monday's first block. Students who refuse to stop on Sunday because they are behind stay behind, because Monday they are tired and angry at the plan. Anger at the plan is how the next weekend is announced even louder and collapses even earlier.",
      "Use a visible timer and a closed door if you have one. If you do not have a door, use a corner and a hat or a sign that means do not ask me for errands until the alarm. Households can respect a sign they have seen before. They cannot respect a mood. Moods are invisible. Signs are dull and they work. Tell one person the end time so the end time exists outside your head.",
      "Homework the teacher set counts as one of the two weekend weaknesses, not as a floating extra. Floating extras sink the finish line. Two weaknesses treated well beat five subjects touched with a highlighter.",
      "Do not add a third subject on Saturday afternoon because you feel guilty about a fourth. Guilt is not a syllabus. Two weaknesses treated well beat five subjects touched. Touching is collapsing in slow motion: many books open, none marked. If a teacher set weekend homework, it counts as one of the two, not as an extra floating duty. Floating duties sink the finish line.",
      "Food and sleep still apply on weekends. A Saturday of skipping meals and a Sunday of staying up to repair Saturday is not a study system. It is a mood swing. Eat ordinary food, sleep at a time that makes Monday possible, and treat a short walk as part of the block, not a betrayal of it. Betrayal language is how students turn rest into a moral problem and then collapse from the moral problem.",
      "If power or noise destroys a block, move the block, do not cancel the weekend. A one-hour Sunday morning session that is marked is a kept plan. A cancelled weekend that you promise to make up next Saturday is how next Saturday inherits two collapses. Inheritance of collapse is the habit you are trying to break. Break it by keeping a smaller line, not by writing a larger poster.",
      "Stop at the finish line even if a cousin has just arrived. Greet the cousin. The books are already closed. Monday's first block is the unfinished page, not a louder Friday speech."
