      "Join points with quiet glue: also, then, however, as a result. Do not write Firstly, secondly through tenthly unless your teacher has told you that the paper likes it. A clean paragraph of connected points often reads more like own words than a numbered clone of the passage's order. That said, do not scramble the order if the passage is a process. Processes have a spine that is sequence. Sequence is not an ornament.",
      "## Word limit as a wall",
      "Drop quotations unless the question asks for the writer's words. Quotation is the opposite of own words. A short embedded phrase may slip in by accident; check and remove it. Names of people and places that carry the point may stay if removing them would invent a different story. Names that only colour the scene should go. Colour is how summaries bloat.",
      "Check that each sentence could be true of a different example. If a sentence only works for the yellow bus in paragraph two, it is still an example. Lift it to the general: public transport failed, or the journey was delayed. Then ask whether that general point is what the question asked. General points that belong to a different question are a new kind of ornament: the clever extra. Clever extras steal words from the spine.",
      "After drafting, read the summary without the passage. If you cannot tell what the original was about, you have dropped too much spine. If you can still see the tomatoes, you have dropped too little ornament. Adjust. This read-without-passage test is faster than arguing with yourself about whether a phrase is fair. Fair is a feeling. The test is a procedure.",
      "Watch grammar in your own sentences. Summary marks often sit beside language marks. A spine written in broken sentences can still be found, but it is a needless risk. Keep subjects and verbs simple. One idea per sentence is allowed. You are not writing a composition to impress. You are writing a reduction that a tired marker can tick.",
