      "Do not perform completion. Map the characters you have actually met, bank quotations you can find on the page, and catch up with page numbers, not with a film. Markers have read the book. They can hear a subplot you borrowed from a classmate.",
      "Draw a character map on one page: names, relationships, and the last thing you personally read them do. Leave blank boxes for unread scenes. Blank boxes are information. Filling them with guesswork from a classmate's recap is how you invent a subplot. Invented subplots sound fluent in oral discussion and die in a contextual question. Contextual questions quote a moment. Moments have addresses in the text.",
      "## Map and quotation bank from pages you own",
      "Build a quotation bank only from pages you have read. Three short lines per major character, copied accurately, with speaker, listener, and a three-word reminder of the situation. Do not copy a page of quotable lines from a pamphlet if you cannot find them in your copy. Pamphlet lines you cannot locate are a liability in an open-book class test and a different liability in a closed-book paper: you will misquote, and misquotation is a kind of fiction the marker did not set.",
      "Read for scenes, not for mood. Mood is what you get from a film summary. Scenes are what papers ask: who is on stage, what is demanded, what changes. After each sitting, write five lines: where, who, what was asked, what changed, one image. Five lines beat a feeling that the chapter was sad. Sad is not a point. A point is that the father refuses the money and the daughter closes the door. Closed doors can be analysed. Sadness without a door cannot.",
      "Use class time as a guided tour of the unread parts, not as a place to hide. When the teacher discusses a later act, write questions in the margin: I have not reached this; what must I know before Friday. Ask after the lesson with the map open. Teachers can point to the two scenes that carry the theme. They cannot, in two minutes, replace the book. Two minutes can stop you from studying the wrong fifty pages first.",
      "An honest catch-up plan has hours and page numbers. Tonight: pages 40 to 65, then the five-line scene note. Tomorrow: 66 to 90. Do not write finish the novel. Finish the novel is a wish that collapses after a house chore. If the exam is close and the book is long, ask the teacher which remaining scenes are essential for the paper you are sitting. Essential is a school judgement, not a rumour from a group chat that the last chapter never comes.",
      "Do not rely on a film or a cartoon summary as the text. Adaptations cut subplots and change endings. If you have already watched one, treat it as a suspect document. Every claim it gave you must be checked against the page when you arrive there. Unchecked film memory is how students write about a scene the board did not print. Boards print books, not films.",
      "Themes can be studied early if you attach them only to evidence you own. If your theme is power, list the two moments you have read where power is used, and leave a space for later moments. In an essay, write I have not yet seen the final confrontation if you are in class practice — and in the real paper, do not offer the final confrontation at all if you still have not read it. Offering it from rumour is fake confidence with ink.",
      "Poetry in the same syllabus should not be ignored because the play is unfinished. A poem you have fully read is a cleaner mark source than a play you have half imagined. Split the week: unfinished prose or drama in short page targets, poems in full. Guilt about the book should not cancel the poems. Cancelled poems are how a literature paper becomes a single bet on a text you are still chasing.",
      "Group reading can help if the group reads aloud from the same edition and stops to do the five-line note. Groups that only discuss how the story will end are guessing clubs. Guessing clubs feel like literature because the talk is fluent. Fluency without page numbers is still fake. Appoint one person to keep the page. The page is the only member who was in the examination board's room.",
      "When a test is announced on a section you have not reached, tell the teacher early, not on the morning of the test. Early honesty can produce a reading order or a passage-based task you can still do. Morning-of honesty produces a scolding and a blank. Use the days you have for the section that will actually be examined if the teacher makes that clear. If they will not, read the assigned pages and write the five-line notes. Assigned pages are the contract.",
      "Keep the map and the bank in the same exercise book so they grow as you read. Date each addition. Dating stops you from treating last month's three quotations as a full warehouse. A warehouse with three boxes is a shelf. Shelves are allowed. Warehouses that you only claimed are not.",
      "Open the unread page. Write five lines: where, who, what was asked, what changed, one image. Add one quotation you can defend. Behind is a location on a map. Maps can be walked."
