      "SBA is not a side quest. Put its deadlines on the same calendar as the board papers. Break projects into dated parts. Keep one board-style question a week even in a cardboard-and-glue week. Copied SBA is peace now and a gap in the hall.",
      "## One calendar",
      "Get the calendar out of your head and onto one sheet. List SBA deadlines, class tests, practical dates, and the national exam window your school has published. If a date is missing, ask once, in writing if needed, and write the answer. A calendar that lives only in a teacher's speech will lose to a louder speech. Paper remembers. You will not remember in the week three projects converge.",
      "Treat each SBA task as a smaller version of the national skill, not as a decoration. A science project that asks you to record observations is training for the practical paper. An oral presentation is training for clear points in an essay. A maths problem set is training for showing working. If you only chase the mark the class teacher gives, you may produce something that looks busy and does not transfer. Transfer is the point of doing both systems in one year.",
      "Break a project into dated parts: question chosen, sources listed, first draft, diagram or data table, final copy, submission. Date the parts on the same calendar as the class tests. A project without parts becomes a Sunday night object, and Sunday night objects collide with revision. Collision is how students then say SBA disturbed my WAEC. The disturbance was the missing parts.",
      "Do not plagiarise a senior's project. Teachers have seen last year's work, and you have not practised the skill. If the school wants originality, originality means your data, your site, your mistakes corrected. Borrowing a title is sometimes allowed; borrowing a results table is not. If you are unsure, ask the teacher what must be yours. That question is part of academic work. Silence is not a method.",
      "Class tests deserve a short, honest revision, not a full mock-exam week. Two evenings of the tested topic, with a marked exercise, are enough for most weekly tests. The national exam needs the leftover evenings. Students who over-prepare every class test arrive at the board papers exhausted and under-practised on paper two. Exhaustion is not loyalty. Loyalty is a calendar that names both.",
      "Keep one folder or one section of a bag for SBA evidence: marked tests, practical sheets, draft feedback. National exam revision needs those sheets because they are a map of your errors. Throwing them away after the class mark is recorded is throwing away a past paper that was written on your exact weaknesses. Weaknesses repeat across school tests and board papers more often than students like to admit. When deadlines clash, rank by immovable date and by weight, then tell the teacher early if a clash is real. Early is a week, not the morning of submission. Teachers can shift a class deadline more often than a board date. They cannot shift what they do not hear. A polite, specific message — I have the practical book due Thursday and a full trial on Friday; may I submit the draft Wednesday — is adult. Adult is the tone SBA is trying to teach alongside the content.",
      "Group SBA is a special risk. Groups hide passengers. If you are the passenger, you will meet the topic alone later. If you are the worker, you will resent the project and avoid the subject. Split the visible tasks on paper with names. Names on paper are not unkind. They are how a group remains a group. Meet once with the parts combined before the deadline so the join is not a night of glue and panic.",
      "## A weekly board-style minimum",
      "Practical books want dates, observations, and a conclusion that matches the data you wrote, not the data you wished. Wishful practicals fail viva questions and train you to invent on the national practical. Inventing is a habit with a short life. Write what happened, including the result that did not match the textbook, and add a sensible reason. Sensible reasons are science. Perfect tables copied from a friend are theatre.",
      "Keep the national exam in view with a weekly minimum that SBA cannot cancel: one timed question in the board style, marked. If a project week is heavy, the minimum can be thirty minutes. Thirty minutes keeps the command words alive. Command words go stale when every evening is cardboard and glue. Cardboard and glue can be scheduled. Stale command words cannot be recovered the night before paper one.",
      "After each SBA returns, spend ten minutes converting the teacher's comment into a board-paper action: show working, label the diagram, answer the question asked. Write that action in the national exam notebook. This is how school marks become board preparation instead of a separate religion. Separate religions fight. One notebook with two calendars can cooperate.",
      "Submit, then return to the weekly board-style minimum. The project is finished when it has left your hands, not when you are still decorating a cover to delay the next topic."
